ZyperOS: the ARM64 JIT's block layout, from
Deadstation Portable (github.com/tyrannotorus/emulator-deadstation-portable, GPL-2.0-or-later as PPSSPP),
a PPSSPP v1.20.4 fork for the TrimUI Brick: its first commit fbde7d6 and commit a205147.

- What a block rarely or never runs lives in a cold area at the top of the code space (4 MB of the
  16): an exit's stub until the exit is linked, out of time, a jump to a block not compiled yet, the
  bail at a block's checked entry. The hot code keeps one branch for each (a conditional one through
  a trampoline after the block, as a conditional branch only reaches 1 MB). The hot code reaches
  the instruction side only after those branches are written (a205147).
- A jump to a register (jr, jalr) runs the dispatcher's fast path in place, so each jump gets a
  branch prediction of its own instead of all sharing one indirect branch.
- A forward link goes past the target's downcount check (its normal entry): only a backward jump
  can make a loop. A block that goes away sends such links back through its checked entry first.
- A forward conditional branch, mostly not taken, puts its not-taken exit first, to fall through.
- The checked entry is one not-taken branch (useForwardJump) instead of three instructions.

Left out of the fork's JIT: its 10 more statically allocated registers, its per-block VFPU prefix
mode, its sine table replacement (not bit-exact) and its test switches.

